<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<databaseChangeLog
        xmlns="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog"
        xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
        xsi:schemaLocation="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog
            http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog/dbchangelog-4.29.xsd">

    <!--
      Restore FORCE ROW LEVEL SECURITY on attendance_quarantine.

      I dropped it in 034 so the backfill could run, and did not put it back. Measured
      immediately after the migration finally succeeded:

          relname                | relforcerowsecurity
          employees              | t
          departments            | t
          designations           | t
          attendance_quarantine  | f          <== mine

      That is a real regression, not a cosmetic one. Without FORCE, hr_user — which OWNS the
      table — is exempt from its own row-level-security policy, so every query hr-service makes
      against attendance_quarantine sees every tenant's rows. It is the exact defect that leaked
      33 tables across 16 tenants on this project earlier, reintroduced on one table by the fix
      for a different bug.

      A NEW changeset rather than an edit to 034, because 034 has already been applied and
      recorded; editing it now would fail Liquibase's checksum validation on every database that
      has it. On a fresh install 034 drops FORCE and this restores it, so both paths end in the
      same state.

      015b does this correctly — it restores FORCE inside its own DO block, before the transaction
      commits, so a failure anywhere rolls the whole thing back and the table is never left
      unprotected even momentarily. 034 should have done the same. The difference between the two
      is the entire lesson: an invariant suspended for a migration has to be restored by the same
      unit of work that suspended it, or it depends on someone remembering.

      ALTER TABLE ... FORCE is idempotent, so this is safe to apply to a database where the table
      is already forced.
    -->
    <changeSet id="hr-1.0.0-035-restore-force-rls-attendance-quarantine" author="restaurantos-agent">
        <sql splitStatements="true" endDelimiter=";">
            ALTER TABLE attendance_quarantine FORCE ROW LEVEL SECURITY;
        </sql>
        <rollback>
            <!-- Deliberately empty. There is no honest inverse: reverting this would re-open a
                 cross-tenant read on a table that holds unmatched attendance records, and no
                 rollback of a later changeset needs that. -->
            <empty/>
        </rollback>
    </changeSet>

</databaseChangeLog>
